  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Humanitarian Multisectoral Response to the Needs of People on the Move (Including Returnees) in Venezuela with a Lifesaving and Human Security Approach</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Venezuela continues experiencing a protracted crisis with critical humanitarian needs despite the recent modest improvement in macroeconomic indicators. According to the 2024-2025 Humanitarian Response Plan extension, the humanitarian situation in Venezuela continues to have an impact on food security, health, protection, water, sanitation and hygiene, education and shelter for over 5.1 million people, who are estimated that might need some type of humanitarian assistance. Although the growth trend is expected to continue in 2024 in some regions of the country, the upcoming electoral period might have an impact on the economy in case of civil or political unrest. Additionally, thousands of people suffer annually due to increasingly frequent disasters of natural origin, such as floods, landslides, droughts, and other man-made hazards. This intervention has three main pillars: the first one focuses on addressing the needs of vulnerable migrants (including returnees) in border areas; the second one on internal displacement in mining and remote areas; and the third one aims to provide tailored assistance to victims of trafficking in a context of human mobility.</narrative>
  </description>
